WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490 line:84%
MICHAEL WARD: His
stories are like nets

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:06.600 line:84%
in which you catch for a moment
something ineffable, something

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.790 line:90%
almost incommunicable.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.290 line:90%
But he has communicated it.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.160 line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.675 line:84%
C.S. LEWIS: In Greek, there are
four words for love: storge...

00:00:14.675 --> 00:00:16.050 line:84%
MICHAEL WARD: C.S.
Lewis was just

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:20.682 line:84%
a master of language he knew
how to point beyond words.

00:00:20.682 --> 00:00:22.390 line:84%
C.S. LEWIS: The eros
is much harder . . .

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.098 line:84%
MICHAEL WARD: The
great thing about Lewis

00:00:24.098 --> 00:00:26.490 line:84%
was that he was creative
and imaginative,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.050 line:84%
as well as being highly
intellectual and analytical.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.310 line:84%
He is so skillful at
expressing the inexpressible.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:36.320 line:84%
What you'll get in this
course is an insight

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.050 line:84%
into how Lewis understood
Christianity and wrote

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.140 line:84%
about it, both in his
nonfiction and his fiction.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.650 line:84%
He knows what it is like to
have been an atheist, been

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.840 line:84%
an unbeliever, been
a skeptic, and he

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.300 line:84%
knows what it's like
to have come to faith.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.300 line:84%
Lewis had thought his
way into all corners

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.590 line:90%
of the Christian faith--

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:59.930 line:84%
pain, joy, love, miracles,
prayer, heaven, hell.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:03.320 line:84%
We're going to cover the
whole gamut of experience.

00:01:05.808 --> 00:01:07.850 line:84%
That's the great thing
about Lewis's presentation

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:08.480 line:90%
of the faith.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.510 line:84%
He makes it live, he makes
it sing, he makes it move,

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:12.740 line:90%
he gives it color.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.270 line:84%
That, I think, is
one of the things

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:16.110 line:90%
that people so love about Lewis.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.320 line:84%
They feel like they're
not just reading books,

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.180 line:84%
they're reading life, and
life with a capital L.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.970 line:84%
LARRY P. ARNN: C.S. Lewis
explained to the world

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:32.740 line:84%
in beautiful English, in common
sense language why Christianity

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:37.860 line:90%
is both good and plausible.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.110 line:84%
MICHAEL WARD: When you
come to Lewis's writings,

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.060 line:84%
it's like you've
touched a live wire.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.100 line:84%
You've suddenly got
an electric shock

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.920 line:84%
because here's someone who
really believes it and manages

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.430 line:84%
to communicate how
much it means to him.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.170 line:90%
I'm Michael Ward.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:54.110 line:84%
This is "C.S. Lewis
on Christianity."

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:55.885 line:90%
Let's begin!

